HOW_TO_READ = ("<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (dashed border = interface; &laquo;record&raquo; = a small immutable "
 "value; &laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed list). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. Bottom: its methods. <b>The arrows.</b> "
 "Hollow triangle = implements. Filled diamond = owns: the limiter owns one KeyState per key and is the only thing that "
 "deletes one. Plain arrow = references or returns. Dashed lavender = handed in through <code>configure()</code>. Dotted blue "
 "= notifies, after the unlock. <b>Where state lives:</b> everything mutable is in KeyState &mdash; the tokens, the instant "
 "they were measured at, the window counters, the log, and the lock that guards all of them. The RateLimiter holds only the "
 "map of those states plus the three things it was handed. Rule and Decision are immutable, so they are shared freely and "
 "never locked. The algorithms hold no state at all: they are handed a KeyState and a Rule and do arithmetic on them, which "
 "is why one instance serves every key in the process.")

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ("Read the green comments above each class and method first &mdash; they say what the thing is for and what it "
 "guarantees, and that is the whole shape of the system in two minutes; then read the bodies for the mechanics. Each copy "
 "button copies that whole file: <code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> "
 "must print ALL PASS, and <code>java Main</code> runs the demo and the hundred-thread race.")

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3><button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div>'
 '<div class="cb"><div class="prompt">"Build me a rate limiter. It sits in front of our API: given a key &mdash; a tenant, an API key &mdash; it says whether this call may go through right now, and it has to be right when a hundred threads ask about the same key at the same instant. I want working code, not a diagram. Go."</div>'
 'Before typing, write your six clarifying questions. Then type in this order: the Clock interface with a manual one for tests; the Rule and Decision records; KeyState with its own lock; the RateLimitAlgorithm interface and TokenBucket behind it; RuleResolver with a flat implementation; RateLimiter with its map, the lock-per-key helper and the charge-then-admit order in <code>allow</code>; the filter that turns a Decision into 429; and a main that fires a hundred threads at one key on a frozen clock.</div></div>')

FU = [
("Ops doubles a tenant's limit during a sale, and a new tenant must ramp up instead of starting at full speed. No restart.", "functional", 5,
 "The limits already live behind <code>RuleResolver</code>, so neither of these opens the limiter. TierRules keeps each tier's rule in an AtomicReference, so <code>setRule</code> takes effect on the next decision and never in the middle of one &mdash; which is why <code>allow</code> reads the rule exactly once into a local. The subtlety is lowering a limit: a bucket may be holding more tokens than the new burst permits, so <code>allow</code> clamps it down before charging, otherwise you have granted permits the new policy says do not exist. The ramp is a wrapper round the same interface: WarmUpRules remembers when it first saw a key and scales the rule's numbers from a tenth up to the whole limit over thirty seconds, leaving the tier rules underneath untouched.",
 sect(src, "class TierRules", "interface LimiterObserver") + "\n" +
 "// the two lines in allow() that make a live change safe\n"
 "Rule rule = rules.ruleFor(key);                          // read ONCE: one decision sees one rule\n"
 "if (s.tokens > rule.burst()) s.tokens = rule.burst();    // burst lowered: hold no more than the new one\n\n" +
 X("warm-up", "a fifth algorithm")),

("A hundred threads hit one key at its limit at the same instant. Prove that exactly the limit gets through.", "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between reading \"one token left\" and writing \"zero\". In this design the refill, the check and the charge happen inside one lock, and that lock is on the key's own state, so no other thread can run in the gap. The proof: a hundred threads wait on one latch with the clock frozen so no permit can be minted mid-race, the latch opens, every thread calls allow for the same key, and the test counts the admissions. Exactly fifty on a limit of fifty, and the bucket ends at zero rather than negative. The clock must be frozen for the assertion to be exact; with a live clock the answer is fifty-or-a-few-more and the test is worthless.",
 T("        // 3. a hundred threads", "        // 4. a key nobody has seen")),

("One lock per key. Does that scale, or have you just moved the bottleneck? What about a single hot key?", "non-functional", 10,
 "It scales, and the answer is arithmetic rather than opinion. The locked section is a subtraction, a multiply, a compare and two field writes: about two tenths of a microsecond, with no I/O and no allocation in it. Twenty thousand requests a second over five thousand keys means the hottest key sees five hundred a second, so its lock is busy a hundred microseconds in every million, and the other keys never touch it &mdash; the map that hands the states out is a ConcurrentHashMap, which has no single lock either. On one genuinely hot key, a hundred threads at the same instant make the last one wait twenty microseconds, against two milliseconds for the request it is gating. If that ever mattered, rung one of the ladder removes the lock entirely: hold the bucket as one immutable value and swap it with compare-and-set, so a thread that loses simply recomputes against the winner's value. The demo prints how often that happens.",
 sect(src, "    public Decision allow(String key, long cost)", "    void refund(String key") + "\n" + X("rung one of the ladder", "shadow mode")),

("The limiter said yes, and then the call never happened — the downstream service threw, or a later check refused it. What happens to the permit?", "functional", 5,
 "It is given back. The order inside allow is charge first, then admit, because admitting is the irreversible half; that leaves exactly one thing to undo, and <code>refund</code> is it. A refund refills from elapsed time first and then adds the permits back capped at the burst, so it can never mint budget that was not earned, and an unknown key is ignored rather than created. The place this really shows up is layered limits: a tenant budget and a global budget charged in order, where a refusal by the global one refunds the tenant &mdash; otherwise the tenant loses permits for a request nobody served. The demo checks that exact number: three admitted through a global budget of three, and the tenant still holding seven of its ten.",
 sect(src, "    void refund(String key", "    long remaining(String key)") + "\n" + X("layered limits", "back-pressure")),

("A dashboard asks \"how many permits are left\" a thousand times a second, and there are ten million keys. Show me both.", "non-functional", 5,
 "The read is O(1) and needs no new structure: the bucket already holds the tokens and the instant they were measured, so the answer is a refill and a floor, under the key's lock for the moment it takes. A read that refills is still a write, which is why it takes the lock; an unknown key answers with a full burst and is deliberately not created, so a monitoring sweep cannot fill the map. Memory is the real question: about two hundred and fifty bytes a key, so a megabyte at five thousand keys and two and a half gigabytes at ten million. So a scheduled task calls sweepIdle, which drops any key nobody has asked about for at least a window. That is lossless, and saying why is the senior move: a key idle for a whole window has already refilled to its full burst, so a fresh state is the same state. The one trap is deleting a key while another thread is deciding on it &mdash; the state is marked removed under the lock, and a caller that finds a removed state fetches again.",
 sect(src, "    long remaining(String key)", "    int sweepIdle(long idleMs)") + "\n" +
 sect(src, "    int sweepIdle(long idleMs)", "    int size()") + "\n" +
 sect(src, "    private KeyState lockState(String key", "    private void publish(String key")),

("Someone spends their whole minute at 11:59:59 and their whole next minute at 12:00:00. Show me the bug and the fix.", "design", 10,
 "A fixed window is a counter plus the window it belongs to, and windows sit on an absolute grid &mdash; they reset on the second, the minute, the hour &mdash; so a client that knows the boundary can spend twice the limit across it. The test proves it: ten calls through in a hundred milliseconds on a limit of five a second. The fix that stays O(1) is the sliding window counter: count this window exactly, and charge a share of the previous window weighted by how much of it is still inside the last sixty seconds. One millisecond after the boundary the previous window counts almost in full, so the same burst is refused. It is approximate by a few percent and it is what most production limiters actually run. The exact answer, a timestamp per permit in a deque, is in the same file and costs memory proportional to the limit; keep it for a login endpoint.",
 sect(src, "class SlidingWindowCounter", "class SlidingWindowLog") + "\n" + T("        // 2. the fixed window", "        // 3. a hundred threads")),

("We run twelve pods. Right now each one grants the full limit, so the tenant gets twelve times what they pay for. Fix it.", "twist", 10,
 "The interface survives; only an implementation is added. A Redis-backed limiter runs the identical refill-and-deduct as one Lua script, and Redis's single thread is the fleet's version of the per-key lock &mdash; the whole point is that the read and the write happen on the server, so there is no gap for twelve pods to race in. The reference code models that with a map behind one lock and the real script in the comment above it; the demo runs two pods against one budget of fifty and admits fifty, not a hundred. Three things to say out loud: keep the local bucket in front as a cheap first filter so most refusals never leave the pod; the TTL on the key is the sweeper, so eviction comes free; and decide now whether a Redis outage fails open or fails closed, because that decision will be made at three in the morning otherwise.",
 X("twelve pods", "a limit per endpoint")),

("Different limits per endpoint, and a search costs more than a ping.", "twist", 5,
 "Both are smaller than they look. \"A limit per endpoint\" is a different key: tenant plus route, so the map grows by the number of routes and not one class changes. \"A search costs more\" is the cost argument that has been on <code>allow</code> since the first line, read from a small table of route to permits; a refusal for a cost of five reports a retry-after long enough to have earned five, not one. The thing worth flagging is cardinality: tenants times routes is the number the eviction sweep now has to keep up with, and it is the reason the sweeper exists rather than a nice-to-have.",
 X("a limit per endpoint", "warm-up")),

("Two things ops asks for before you switch it on: a dry run that blocks nobody, and a way for a batch job to wait instead of fail.", "twist", 10,
 "Both are wrappers around the same interface, and neither touches the bucket. Shadow mode runs the real algorithm and charges the real permits, then admits everything anyway and counts what it would have blocked; a week of that number on a dashboard is how you answer \"what will this break?\" honestly, and switching it on is one wiring line. Waiting is the other direction: the decision already carries a retry-after, so a caller that would rather wait loops around the same call, sleeping exactly that long, until a deadline it chose. Say the warning with it: never offer that to a web request, because held threads under load are how a service falls over. It is for batch jobs and queue consumers.",
 X("shadow mode", "layered limits") + "\n" + X("back-pressure", "twelve pods")),

("Where does time come from, and how do you test a refill without a Thread.sleep?", "design", 5,
 "Time is handed in as a one-method interface. In production it is System.nanoTime rather than the wall clock, because only elapsed time matters here and nanoTime cannot jump backwards when NTP corrects the machine. In tests it is a clock the test sets, so \"two hundred milliseconds later\" is one line that runs instantly; the whole suite has no sleep in it. That is also what makes the nasty cases testable: the clock going backwards must mint nothing, and it must not mint anything for the interval it travelled twice either. The belt-and-braces clamp is still in the refill &mdash; elapsed of zero or less mints nothing &mdash; because a monotonic clock is a promise about production, not about whatever a caller injects.",
 sect(src, "interface Clock", "enum Tier") + "\n" + T("        // 1. burst then refill", "        // 2. the fixed window")),

("Which pattern is where, and why did each one earn its place?", "design", 5,
 "None was chosen up front; each is what a move produced. Strategy is move 3: the algorithm is a one-call interface handed in through configure, so the sliding window is a new class and one changed line. Decorator appears twice, both times wrapping rather than copying: shadow mode wraps a whole limiter and swallows its refusals, and the warm-up rule wraps a rule source and scales its numbers. Observer is move 4's consequence that nothing slow may sit inside the lock, so the limiter announces every decision after the unlock and has no idea what a metric is. State is move 6: FRESH, SPENDING, THROTTLED, IDLE, which is what forced the charge-then-admit order. Rule and Decision are value objects: immutable, shared by every key on a tier, nothing to lock. Factory has not earned a place &mdash; it earns one the day algorithms come from a config string and a registry maps the name to a constructor. Singleton earns nothing at all: one limiter per process is a wiring fact, and a static getInstance would make every test share a bucket.",
 "// Strategy: the rule that decides, behind an interface, handed in\n"
 "interface RateLimitAlgorithm { Decision tryAcquire(KeyState s, Rule rule, long cost, long nowMs); }\n"
 "void configure(RateLimitAlgorithm algo, RuleResolver rules, Clock clock) { ... }\n\n"
 "// Decorator: wrap the thing, do not copy it\n"
 "class ShadowLimiter implements Limiter { private final Limiter delegate; /* run it, then allow anyway */ }\n"
 "class WarmUpRules implements RuleResolver { private final RuleResolver base; /* scale its numbers by age */ }\n\n"
 "// Observer: announced after the unlock, each listener in its own try/catch\n"
 "private void publish(String key, Decision d) {\n"
 "    for (LimiterObserver o : observers) { try { o.onDecision(key, d); } catch (RuntimeException ignored) { } }\n"
 "}\n\n"
 "// State: a key's life, and the order it forces inside allow()\n"
 "// FRESH -> SPENDING -> THROTTLED -> SPENDING -> IDLE (swept).  Charge, then admit. Refund undoes the charge.\n\n"
 "// Factory: not yet. It earns its place the day this line is needed:\n"
 "Map<String, Supplier<RateLimitAlgorithm>> registry = Map.of(\"token_bucket\", TokenBucket::new, \"sliding_log\", SlidingWindowLog::new);\n"
 "RateLimitAlgorithm algo = registry.get(config.get(\"algorithm\")).get();\n"),

("Which SOLID letter is where in this code?", "design", 5,
 "S: one reason to change each. TokenBucket changes when token arithmetic changes, RateLimiter when the key lifecycle changes, TierRules when the plans change, MetricsCounter when counting changes; none of them does two of those. O: shadow mode and the second global budget were both new classes and one wiring line, and TokenBucket &mdash; the class most likely to hide a concurrency bug &mdash; was never reopened. L: every Limiter promises the same three things (never admit more than the rule allows, never block, always return a usable retry-after), which is exactly why LayeredLimiter can hold a mixture of them with no instanceof anywhere. I: Clock, RuleResolver and LimiterObserver are one method each, so a test double is a lambda. D: the limiter is handed its clock, its rules and its algorithm, which is why a test can give it a clock that jumps and a listener that throws.",
 "// S: one reason to change each\n"
 "class TokenBucket { /* token arithmetic */ }   class RateLimiter { /* keys, locks, order */ }   class TierRules { /* policy */ }\n\n"
 "// O: new behaviour is a new class plus one wiring line; nothing was opened\n"
 "Limiter live = new ShadowLimiter(limiter);                 // day 1: measure\n"
 "Limiter live = limiter;                                    // day 8: enforce\n\n"
 "// L: a mixture of implementations, and not one instanceof\n"
 "new LayeredLimiter(List.of(new Layer(\"tenant\", perTenant, k -> k), new Layer(\"global\", global, k -> \"ALL\")));\n\n"
 "// I: one method each, so a fake is a lambda\n"
 "interface Clock { long nowMs(); }   interface RuleResolver { Rule ruleFor(String key); }\n\n"
 "// D: handed in, which is what makes the nasty tests possible\n"
 "limiter.configure(new TokenBucket(), new FlatRules(Rule.perSecond(5)), manualClock);\n"
 "limiter.addObserver((k, d) -> { throw new RuntimeException(\"the metrics sidecar is down\"); });\n"),

("An enum for the algorithm with a switch, or an interface? And where would a Factory actually pay here?", "design", 3,
 "Split it by whether the thing is data or behaviour. A tier is data &mdash; FREE, PRO and ENTERPRISE differ only in three numbers &mdash; so it is an enum with a rule per constant, and adding a plan is one constant and one row. An algorithm is behaviour, with its own fields and its own arithmetic, so an enum with a switch inside <code>tryAcquire</code> would put four unrelated implementations in one method and reopen it for every new one; that is an interface, and the sliding window proved it by arriving as a class rather than a case. A Factory pays the moment creation stops being a constructor call: algorithms named in a YAML file, a registry from string to constructor, so a new one is a registration rather than a growing switch. Until then it is one <code>new</code> in the wiring code and a factory would only hide it.",
 "// data: a tier is three numbers, so it is an enum plus a table\n"
 "enum Tier { FREE, PRO, ENTERPRISE }\n"
 "rules.setRule(Tier.PRO, Rule.perMinute(600, 100));\n\n"
 "// behaviour: an algorithm has its own state and its own arithmetic, so it is a class behind an interface\n"
 "class SlidingWindowCounter implements RateLimitAlgorithm { /* arrived without reopening anything */ }\n\n"
 "// NOT this: one method that must be reopened for every new algorithm\n"
 "enum Algo { TOKEN_BUCKET, FIXED_WINDOW;\n"
 "    Decision tryAcquire(...) { switch (this) { case TOKEN_BUCKET: ...; case FIXED_WINDOW: ...; } } }\n\n"
 "// the Factory earns its place when the name comes from configuration\n"
 "Map<String, Supplier<RateLimitAlgorithm>> registry = Map.of(\"token_bucket\", TokenBucket::new, \"gcra\", Gcra::new);\n"),
]

